h.append('<h2 id="s1">1. Normalization and equal-depth sensitivities</h2><p>The original featureCounts matrix has43,794 fixed merged peaks;16,046 have any count across eight libraries. All three continuous normalization comparisons use those same16,046 IDs without directional filtering. The original score uses log2 ratios of mean CPM with a0.5 pseudocount. Existing QN applies full mapped-library CPM, log2(CPM+0.5), then preprocessCore quantile normalization. The new calculation instead uses log2(raw count+0.5), then QN directly across eight libraries. For QN, mean transformed CLIP minus mean transformed SMI defines each construct enrichment; delta-delta is324 minus328. Sparse tied values retain averaged reference ranks.</p>')
h.append(cfgfig('QNRAW'));h.append(figure('output/followup_analysis/figures/region_intron_effects.png','Same16046 peak universe and raw-count QN. Each point is Cliff’s delta against the934 intron peaks; positive means more324-favoring relative to intron. Regions with at least20 peaks are tested. Peak-level exploratory Wilcoxon/KS tests do not account for gene-level dependence; BH spans the displayed comparisons.'))
h.append(small(out/'tables/region_vs_intron.tsv',['grouping','region','n','n_intron','median','median_difference','cliffs_delta','q_BH','ks_D']))
for stem in ['all_region_density','all_region_ecdf']:
 h.append(figure('output/followup_analysis/figures/'+stem+'.png','Fixed16046 peaks;raw-count log2+0.5 then QN;one weight per peak. Curves are normalized within each region: probability density or cumulative fraction of324-minus328 delta-delta. No directional selection.'))
h.append('<p>The relative3′UTR/intron effect survives but is small: median difference−0.184 and Cliff’s delta−0.120. CDS has the same median as intron after tied-rank normalization; its distribution can still differ in shape. MixedUTR/CDS has619 peaks and is included. The9 unassigned detailed-category peaks fail the prespecified minimum20 and are not individually tested.</p><h3>Repeated exact read subsampling</h3><p>We sampled whole eligible aligned records without replacement50 times (seed260921). CLIP is matched to the sparse324CLIP depth93,659. The smallest SMI library is328, so SMI is separately matched to257,457; deeper324SMI libraries are also reduced. Each replicate remains separate. Counts are reconstructed from round-two per-read memberships, which retain every overlapping peak; featureCounts did not use the multi-overlap -O option. The reconstructed full-depth baseline therefore differs slightly from the original matrix, and the saved QC table quantifies this. All16,046 fixed IDs remain supported at full depth. Resampling comparisons use that reconstructed full-depth baseline, holding its counting rule constant.</p>')
h.append(small(out/'tables/downsampling_depths.tsv',['sample_id','construct','assay','biological_replicate','original_depth','target_depth','iterations','seed']));h.append(cfgfig('DOWNSAMPLE'));h.append(small(out/'tables/downsampling_region_summary.tsv'))
h.append('<p>Counts are converted to CPM using the selected library depth before the established ratio-of-mean-CPM score. This removes unequal assay depth by random selection while preserving a common scale between CLIP and SMI. The draws are technical sensitivity iterations, not50 biological replicates. The repeated identical3′UTR/intron medians reflect sparse count ties; they are not evidence of zero uncertainty for all peaks. Per-peak intervals and direction-preservation fractions are saved.</p>')
h.append('<h2 id="s2">2. Read-level transcript annotation</h2><p>The source BAMs are unavailable locally, but round two exported every eligible primary mapped alignment once, including position,strand,CIGAR and all overlapping categories. Those GENCODEv26 memberships already used alignedM/= /X blocks, excluding N and D; junction-spanning reads therefore do not cover the intervening intron. Reusing these exact annotations avoids another BAM pass. Each read receives1/k for each of its k distinct categories. Transcript ambiguity is not resolved to a single isoform. Empty-gene unassigned records are separated as intergenic; other annotated noncoding/internal exonic material remains other.</p>')
h.append(cfgfig('FRACTIONAL'));h.append(figure('output/followup_analysis/figures/read_nonexclusive_annotation.png','All eight eligible libraries;per-library N in depth table. Numerator: aligned records with at least one block overlapping the category. Denominator: all eligible records in that library. A read may count in several categories;fractions need not sum to1. Points show biological replicatesA andB.'))
h.append('<p>Mean fractional intron allocation is42.4% in324CLIP versus20.8% in328CLIP; corresponding SMI fractions are37.9% and19.8%. Thus much of this compositional difference is also present in the controls. Mean3′UTR allocation is18.9% versus39.0% in CLIP and11.8% versus14.9% in SMI. These are read-composition diagnostics, not estimates of numbers of bound peaks.</p>')
h.append('<h2 id="s3">3. UAG motif validation</h2>');h.append(cfgfig('UAG'));h.append(small(out/'tables/UAG_enrichment.tsv'))
h.append('<p>Use the strand-oriented51nt genomic window centered on the highest-score source summit for each merged peak. All43,794 windows were valid. UAG is TAG in the DNA reference. The null preserves each window’s nucleotide and dinucleotide counts,length,first/last nucleotide and therefore its GC content/context. Randomized outgoing-edge order and an Euler trail generate200 sequence shuffles per window (seed260924); this is not claimed to sample all possible dinucleotide shuffles uniformly. The interval reflects randomization variability. The minimum attainable empirical p is1/201. Central enrichment with approximately neutral flanks supports a localized MSI2 motif positive control; it does not establish an isoform difference.</p>')
h.append(cfgfig('UAG-UTR'));h.append('<p>As an orthogonal ZFP36-style sequence-exposure check, the26,504 UTR-associated summit windows have2.78-fold more UAG per possible trinucleotide start than their full matched splicedUTRs. Each of8,869 UTR models is weighted by its assigned summit count. This adapts the paper’s UTR sequence background;it is an analytic comparison,not a PARpipe shuffle or a new permutation p-value. The maximum positional UAG frequency occurs at motif start−3nt,close to the inferred summit.</p>')
h.append('<h2 id="s4">4. Intronic spatial analyses</h2><p>Inward profiles retain170,277 deduplicated introns at least2kb long. The donor and acceptor windows each contain the first1,000 intronic bases from their respective ends, in10nt bins. Distance0 is the boundary-adjacent intronic nucleotide, and distance increases into the intron for both sides. The windows do not overlap. Every bin has the same170,277-intron exposure; <a href="tables/inward_nonzero_bin_support.tsv">actual nonzero intron counts for every inward bin and library</a> are saved alongside the fixed denominator. <a href="tables/downsampling_support_iterations.tsv">Nonzero peak counts by subsample and library</a> are also retained.</p>')
h.append(cfgfig('INWARD'));h.append(cfgfig('INWARD-RATIO'));h.append(cfgfig('SPATIAL-DD'))
h.append('<p>The existing round-two100-bin intron read metaprofiles are reused as the historical baseline. New sensitivities explicitly require length>=100nt (338,659 introns), stratify by annotation-wide length quartiles and preserve all library curves. Equal coefficients in a mean depth do not prevent high-depth introns from influencing the result, so a second analysis normalizes each supported intron to unit total bin-density before averaging. Zero-support introns cannot be shape-normalized and are reported separately rather than labeled bound. After equal-shape normalization, sharp end-bin peaks occur in bothCLIP andSMI and are strongest for short introns. They therefore cannot be interpreted as MSI2-specific localization without the assay contrast.</p>')
h.append(cfgfig('META-DEPTH'));h.append(cfgfig('META-SHAPE'));h.append(cfgfig('META-READWEIGHT'));h.append(cfgfig('META-SITE'))
h.append('<p>The reference summit analysis assigns each summit to one longest containing intron, separately from UTR assignment. It includes20,542 summits in9,732 selected introns out of338,659 eligible models. First10% contains16.73% of summits versus approximately10% under the matched null;last10% contains8.11%. This relative donor-side preference is broader than an exact junction spike. Per-intron paired zone tests, length-stratified summit tests, construct-by-boundary Fisher tests and read-weighted/equal-intron profiles are all saved; their small p-values reflect many correlated annotation units, not additional biological replicates.</p>')
h.append('<h2 id="s5">5. Length-normalized3′UTR spatial analysis</h2><p>The primary reference analysis contains26,504 summits assigned once to the longest matching spliced3′UTR model of at least100nt. Concatenation excludes intervening introns. The number of sites per selected model,length,strand and gene context remain fixed in500 uniform-position permutations (seed260925). The null does not match local mappability or nucleotide composition. Results are conditional on the chosen transcript models; some loci can be intronic in one transcript and UTR in another, so the intron and UTR analysis sets are not disjoint.</p>')
h.append(cfgfig('UTR'))
for name in ['UTR_summit_histogram','UTR_summit_ECDF']:
 h.append(figure('output/followup_analysis/figures/'+name+'.png','Same26,504 reference summits in eligible spliced3′UTRs. One site weight each. Histogram y-axis=count per1% bin;ECDF y-axis=cumulative fraction,with uniform reference diagonal. Denominator for fractions is26,504;not construct libraries.'))
h.append('<p>The last10% contains12.58% of sites (1.26-fold null) and the final100nt contains7.77% (1.41-fold null). The first10% is also modestly enriched at1.11-fold; the data do not imply exclusive distal binding. These reference-site results remain separate from the following secondary plots, which label counted peaks by the sign of their new raw-QN construct score.</p>');h.append(cfgfig('CONSTRUCT-SITES'))
h.append('<h2 id="s6">6. PARpipe/ZFP36 methods and external RBP controls</h2><p><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4053807/">Mukherjee et al.2014</a> used the longest matching transcript feature and within-feature positional randomization. We inspected <a href="https://github.com/ohlerlab/PARpipe/tree/79b1aabfc985bdf750fa304bd1d217442da940b7">PARpipe commit79b1aab</a>, including Spatial.pl,Spatial.R and annotate.pl. The spatial R function uses an implicit25nt footprint: sample(12.5:(L−12.5),1). We reproduce that grid exactly for500 draws on the current hg38/v26 models. We adapt the coordinate logic,not the historical preprocessing or annotation precedence; the old annotation code contains GENCODEv18/hg19 assumptions. No obsolete aligner or peak caller was run. This is a PARpipe spatial-randomization sensitivity,not a complete PARpipe analysis. No independent motif-shuffling method was identified in these spatial scripts. The paper’s fullUTR motif sequence background is separately adapted above;the primary UAG null remains the explicitly named dinucleotide shuffle.</p>');h.append(cfgfig('PARPIPE'))
h.append('<p>The historical margin changes the numerical null but preserves the direction of both the distal3′UTR and first-decile intron enrichment. It does not independently validate local sequence or mappability assumptions.</p><h3>Published RBP peaks through the same splice annotation</h3><p><a href="../external_rbp_splice_comparison/README.md">Detailed methods and interpretation</a> · <a href="../external_rbp_splice_comparison/DATA_SOURCES.tsv">Data sources</a> · <a href="../external_rbp_splice_comparison/liftover_qc.tsv">Conversion QC</a>. QKI/PUM2 are the exact archived Hafner2010 datasets; ELAVL1A metadata identifies Kishore2011 but does not resolve the library subset. All comparisons use287,085 donors and279,145 acceptors from the original MSI2 landmark file. Downloaded GENCODEv26 reproduces all338,659 existing introns of at least100nt exactly,with no coordinate differences.</p>')
h.append(small(root/'output/external_rbp_splice_comparison/liftover_qc.tsv',['RBP','original_N','unmapped','split_or_multiple','width_QC_rejected','retained_N','percent_retained']))
h.append(figure('output/external_rbp_splice_comparison/external_rbp_splice_comparison.png','MSI2=43,794 summit representatives;QKI=10,958,PUM2=10,027,ELAVL1=10,585 same-strand genic null-eligible hg38 peak centers. One nearest donor/acceptor per peak;10nt bins. Denominator=all null-eligible peaks for that RBP,including distant peaks. Y=observed fraction /mean fraction from500 same-gene/region-segment randomizations (seed260923);grey=95% null interval divided by null mean. Categories/segment,chromosome,strand,peak count and width are held fixed;GC/mappability are not matched. Donornegative/exonpositive/intron;acceptornegative/intronpositive/exon. Inherited acceptor0 is the last intronic nucleotide.'))
h.append(small(root/'output/external_rbp_splice_comparison/splice_zone_summary.tsv'))
h.append(small(root/'output/external_rbp_splice_comparison/splice_proximity_summary.tsv'))
h.append('<p>The same reference MSI2 called peaks are not strongly enriched at the read-derived donor[−10,0) or acceptor[0,20) extrema. Broad exon-side50nt windows are0.92-fold donor and0.84-fold acceptor. MSI2 has some broader similarity toQKI (descriptive correlation0.43/0.48),but not a compelling exact-boundary enrichment. PUM2 andELAVL1 differ;ELAVL1 has an intron-side acceptor feature. All sets show some immediate-boundary depletion,consistent with potential cluster-width/annotation/protocol contributions. This does not prove a general artifact, nor establish MSI2-specific splice regulation.</p><p>Low overall splice-site occupancy is not unusual: MSI2 occupies1.52% of annotated donors within250nt,whileQKI occupies0.36%. Because peak-set sizes differ,also compare fractions of peaks:9.64% of MSI2 and7.78% ofQKI peaks are within250nt of a donor. Both denominators are reported rather than comparing raw peak counts alone.</p>')
h.append(figure('output/splicing_first_pass/figures/donors/site_weighted_enrichment_metaprofile.png','Reused original MSI2 donor read result:101162 condition-independent supported donors;all other donors excluded. Each retained site weighted equally,including zero bins. Counts divided by within-construct full-library factors (depth/geometric mean). Per-pair log2((CLIP+0.1)/(SMI+0.1)) then mean of two pairs;95% bands from1000 primary-gene bootstrap draws,seed328324. Y=mean site log enrichment;denominator101162 sites. This is not called-site O/E.'))
h.append('<p><a href="../splicing_first_pass/README.md">Original first-pass methods</a> and <a href="../analysis_comparison_registry/report.html">the registry</a> retain the original site-weighted/read-weighted variants and their exact denominators. A strong read-coverage feature with weak called-site localization must remain a coverage observation.</p>')
h.append('<h2 id="s7">7. Overall interpretation and method comparison</h2><p>The overall328-favoring fixed-peak score survives both quantile-normalization orders and repeated equal-depth sensitivity. Its magnitude and the regional contrast are method-sensitive,with sparse ties particularly important. The fraction of reads allocated to introns differs between constructs but also differs in SMI,so it is not equivalent to preferential CLIP binding. UAG enrichment is a clear localized specificity check. Relative intron andUTR spatial biases are detectable,whereas the exact exon-side read-profile extrema are weak in called-peak space. These conclusions concern binding localization and normalization robustness,not proof of splicing regulation.</p>')
h.append(table(compare));h.append('<h3>Reproducibility and limitations</h3><p>Tables preserve biological samplesA/B and individual iterations wherever available. Permutation/subsampling bands quantify algorithmic resampling,not biological confidence intervals. Most feature/peak tests are exploratory and do not correct gene/transcript dependence. Source provenance is incomplete for the MSI2 reference and for the preciseELAVL1A library subset. The read exports are aligned-record data,not raw FASTQ;UMI/insert collapsing and mapping filters occurred upstream. Round-two memberships contain ambiguous annotation overlaps and do not establish whole-read transcript compatibility.</p><p><a href="tables/validation_checks.tsv">54 structural/numerical checks plus exact annotation reconciliation</a> verify fixed IDs,read weight conservation,shape mass and liftover accounting. <a href="tables/reconstructed_count_QC.tsv">Reconstructed count QC</a> documents the deliberate read-overlap counting distinction. All figures link toPNG;matchingPDFs and plotting inputs are saved. <a href="README.md">Rebuild instructions</a> identify scripts,reference sources and seeds.</p><details><summary>All new figure metadata</summary>'+table(figs)+'</details></body></html>')
